            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"domain\",\"location\":\"Finset.Icc 1 p\",\"explanation\":\"The Lean proposition uses representatives 1 through p, while the informal problem uses 0 through p−1. These may nevertheless give equivalent statements because F is a polynomial evaluated modulo p.\",\"prediction\":\"Test equivalence with the proposition using the informal interval. Equivalence would show that this domain shift does not change the theorem.\",\"probe_kind\":\"equivalence\",\"comparison\":\"∀ (p : ℕ) (F : ℕ → ℕ), (Odd p ∧ p.Prime) → (∀ n : ℕ, F n = ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (p - 1), (i + 1) * n ^ i) → ∀ a ∈ Finset.Icc 0 (p - 1), ∀ b ∈ Finset.Icc 0 (p - 1), a ≠ b → ¬(F a ≡ F b [MOD p])\",\"witnesses\":null,\"edit_from\":null,\"edit_to\":null,\"bridge\":\"translated\",\"obligation\":\"Review whether the informal integer domain is faithfully represented by natural numbers from 0 through p−1. Even if that translation is accepted, determine whether polynomial periodicity makes the two quantified statements equivalent before treating the changed representatives as a defect.\"}],\"summary\":\"The quantified intervals differ; whether that difference affects the theorem remains to be tested.\"}"
